            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book offers a compelling mechanical explanation for lamp oil that distinguishes it from anointing oil, resolving contradictions in the traditional Holy Spirit interpretation. By defining lamp oil as truth and instruction worked into obedience (the fruit of the Spirit), it explains why the oil in Matthew 25 must be acquired ('bought') over time, why it cannot be shared (character and lived obedience are non-transferable), and why the foolish (who hear but do not do) lack it despite holding the lamp (the commandment, per Prov 6:23). The traditional view struggles to explain how the Holy Spirit could be 'bought' (explicitly forbidden in Acts 8) or hoarded as a zero-sum quantity. The book's use of Zechariah 4 also aligns textually: the olive branches are the 'anointed ones' emptying oil out of themselves, which fits the idea of obedience/fruit produced by God's servants better than the Spirit Himself being emptied out of them.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Truth and correction worked into proven obedience (the tested, pressed fruit of the Spirit) that fuels a visible life of good works.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"tested, pressed fruit\",\n    \"stored as evidence of faithful obedience\",\n    \"burned to give a visible example that lights the Way\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The chapter's assertion that the ten virgins represent the ten northern tribes of Israel based on Ahijah's ten pieces of cloth relies on a broader typological argument not required by the core symbol of oil.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"Lamp oil universally represents the Holy Spirit (His indwelling presence and empowering grace).\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"The Holy Spirit.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"Lamp oil is the Spirit Himself, not the obedience, works, or character produced by the Spirit in the believer.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"Zechariah 4 explains the vision of the oil-supplied lampstand with the decree, 'Not by might, nor by power, but by my Spirit,' creating a direct association between the oil and the Spirit.\",\n    \"Anointing oil in 1 Samuel 16:13 is immediately followed by the Spirit of the LORD coming upon David, establishing oil generally as a symbol of the Spirit.\",\n    \"The foolish virgins lacking oil represents nominal believers who have an outward form of religion but lack the indwelling Holy Spirit.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Preserves the strict scriptural distinction between pure beaten lamp oil and compounded anointing oil (Exodus 25:6, 30:22-33).\",\n    \"Explains why the oil in Matthew 25 cannot be shared (obedience/character is non-transferable, whereas the Spirit is not a finite resource).\",\n    \"Explains why the wise send the foolish to 'buy' it, matching Proverbs 23:23 ('Buy the truth') and Proverbs 21:20 while avoiding the heresy of purchasing the Holy Spirit (Acts 8:20).\",\n    \"Links Proverbs 21:20 ('oil in the dwelling of the wise, but a foolish man spendeth it up') directly to the parable's wise and foolish virgins.\",\n    \"Accounts for the mechanics in Zechariah 4 where the anointed ones empty oil 'out of themselves'—a fitting description of fruit/works produced by God's servants, but an awkward description of the Holy Spirit's origin.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Relies on the most immediately intuitive reading of Zechariah 4:6 ('by my Spirit') as the direct decoder for the lampstand's fuel, without requiring a multi-step derivation.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Proverbs 6:23\",\n    \"Proverbs 21:20\",\n    \"Matthew 25:8-9\",\n    \"Acts 8:18-20\",\n    \"Exodus 25:6\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
